Some of you may have missed the article, by Alison Utley, on the front
page of last Friday's (5th March 1993) 'Times Higher Educational
Supplement' (which is the premiere newspaper for UK University
academics, resrearchers and administrators) entitled 'JANET harassed by
computer porn'. I'll try and summarise it here and leave others to
comment:

--

'Forget plain brown wrappers. Explicit pornography is currently in mass
circulation around universities via the academic computer network, JANET',
it starts.

'Vicky Merchant, of the University of Central Lancashire - the only
full-time sexual harrassment officer in higher education - said highly
offensive graphics and words were being used to displace women's
on-screen work. Ms Merchant has dealt with nine case of sexual
harrassment using pornography since October, and has become so concerned
about the growth of a computer harassment network that a national
seminar has been arranged for later this month'.

'In some cases a woman's computer password is obtained and the
pornography then placed in her storage area. Since the university
defines the storage of pornography anywhere on the computer as sexual
harassment, this serves the dual function of harassing the woman and
keeping the material out of the harasser's own storage area'.

The article goes one to say that Ms Merchant has had cases of 'porn'
being electronically mailled to people on the network, and 'sometimes
printed out' and posted around campus. JANET is being used to 'exchange
porn between institutions, possibly via overseas' according to the
article (I'm not clear if that's a statment of fact or a quote for Ms
Merchant).

'Unlike magazines and videos, monitoring or controlling the movement of
such files is virtually impossible', the article says.

'The seminar, on March 17 at the University of Central Lancashire, will
assess the extent of the problem and look at links between pornography
and sexual violence. Sergent Jon Ashley of Greater Manchester Police
obscene publications department will attend', it ends.

Just for interest, you can probably find out more about this conference
by calling the University of Central Lancashire on (0772) 201201 or
(0772) 892000 (international replace 0772 with +44 772).
