The NAS said nothing about its goal as being one of professional
scholars opposing "radical political indoctrination." The wording was
quite the opposite: The phrasing denoted an organization committed to
the free flow of ideas and opposing "right-think." However, to accomplish
openness, it would seem to practice exclusion.  John Higdon perhaps
misses the irony of his post:  The NAS appears to be an organization
ostensibly dedicated to the free flow of ideas while simultaneously
striving to restrict those with differing ideas.  This is intellectual
fraud.

If John Higdon is correct in his claim that the NAS is dedicated to
opposing a set of ideas it finds politically objectionable, and if
he is correct in his assessing the NAS policy as viewing competing
views as suicidal, chaotic, or absurd, then he should also
recognize that the NAS is a well-organized forum for stifling
freedom of speech in the name of academic freedom. The
NAS, judging from his and the organization's own blurb, seems
the ultimate in political correctness. The rallying point is not
the intellectual substance, theoretical cogency, or empirical
validity of ideas, but rather a political litmus test.

Jim Thomas

