And why, may I ask, would anyone think this wrong?  How else can a library
enforce its return policy?

If you check out a book, you are responsibly for it, even if someone steals
it from you.  This is the case whenever something is lent or leased -- the
person or party that acquires the item implicitly accepts responsibility
for its return.  When returned, it must be in or within an agreed-upon
condition.

If a library can't assume you're return or pay for the books you check out,
then they'd have to assume they're going to lose money, and that it isn't
worth providing the service (I certainly wouldn't want my taxes going to
a public library system that simply GAVE AWAY books).

From the original thread under this subject, I'd guess the reason feel it's
wrong for a library to disallow continuing check-out when other books are
overdue is because this violates "due process".  Folks, due process applies
to our legal system, and is not required to apply to every other aspect of
our lives.  Certain trade-offs have to be made in order to keep the process
at a reasonable level.  The reason due process is so important with regard
to the legal system is that so much is at stake!  Yes, it'd be nice if we
could have due process for everything, but that's unreasonable.

Looking at the library scenerio, what's going to happen in either case, when
someone fails to return a book on time because it was (say) stolen?

[without due process]  The individual is unable to check out any books until
he pays for the one stolen (since he is responsible for it).  Since he can
take as long as he cares to pay the fine, he is soley responsible for the
duration of the punishment.

[with due process]  The individual presents an explaination, and attends a
hearing of several library staffers who review his claim that the book was
stolen.  They unanimously agree that, because of the agreement made when the
person's library card was issued, the person is responsible for the book, and
is fined the price of it.  Given the time and effort required for the library
staff to attend the hearing, it would be reasonable to allow them to require
the person to pay the fine immedeately, or suffer the revocation of his
library privileges.  If the person refuses, they revoke his privileges, and
require he return all other outstanding books (regardless of due date).

In the first case, the library (and the remainder of the public) is without
a book for as long as the person who checked it out refuses to pay the fine.
The person still has any other books he had checked out, and suffers no
penalty on them unless he fails to return them on time.  Punishment is
minimal, and responsibility for the loss is placed squarely on the party
responsible for the safe return of the book.

 Steve "Stevers!" Coile                    scoile@gmuvax[{.BITNET|[2].gmu.edu}]

