The issue is "fair use".  E.g., it is fair use for me to generate
an image of Snoopy, and staple it up on my daughter's wall.  It
would not be fair use for me to generate an image of Snoopy, and
sell it, or give it away in trade, or position it in such a way
that it could be considered an endorsement of a product or
service, mine or anyone elses.

What constitutes "trade" is a reasonably muddy grey area.  Two
events happened locally in recent years:

A woodworker in Vermont made himself a tall threelegged stool,
painted it white with black spots, and attached a pink
uddershaped thingy to the bottom of the seat.  It was a novelty
display piece for his mill.

Someone came along and paid him an incredible amount of money for
the stool.  So he decided to go into production and sell the
things in the various outlets for his mill.  He also was granted
and received a copyright (Yes, a copyright) for this, as a "work
of art".  Shortly after these stools hit national distribution,
someone made a competing line and attempted to market them;  our
Vermonter successfully defended his copyright.


Not too far from where I sit, there's a small bar and pizza joint
that is now called Mousetrap Pizza.  It used to be Mickey's Mouse
Trap (the owner was, indeed, named Michael), but a spotter for
Disney Studios took exception to his sign, which did, indeed,
have an image of a potbellied mouse wearing shorts secured by two
