In article <1993Mar05.015309.18249@clarinet.com> brad@clarinet.com (Brad Templeton) writes:

>A site, I feel, is liable only if it knows it has a group full of pirated
>stuff (that means you, I just told you that you do) and decides not to
>delete it.

I think this places much too great a burden on news servers.  Recall that the
typical NetNews provider does not sift through the material.  In some sense,
the material is provided "as is."  The provider just provides access--it
takes no responsibility for what is contained in the news itself.  (Those
providers who do sift through the material, edit it, etc., open themselves
up to much greater liability.)

This is distinctly different from, say, a typical BBS, where uploads are
cataloged, placed into an appropriate directory, and otherwise processed
by the sysop.

The scheme that you suggests would mean that a NetNews provider would have
to look into every claim that a particular article is problematic--whether
the problem be possible copyright violation, or whatever.  Under such a
scheme, the amount of work necessary to maintain a news server could, very
quickly, become completely unmanageable.


