In article <199303090438.AA07905@eff.org> TK0JUT1@NIU.BITNET (Jim Thomas                   , tk0jut1@niu.bitnet) writes:

>If John Higdon is correct in his claim that the NAS is dedicated to
>opposing a set of ideas it finds politically objectionable, and if
>he is correct in his assessing the NAS policy as viewing competing
>views as suicidal, chaotic, or absurd, then he should also
>recognize that the NAS is a well-organized forum for stifling
>freedom of speech in the name of academic freedom

I can understand being careful, and skeptical, but it really seems to me that
all of this conjecture about the NAS has really gone off of the deep end.

From what I know of the NAS, it is clear to me that they are not opposing
any set of ideas that they find politically objectionable.  What they seek
to do is

  1) Eliminate the addition of courses for the sole purpose of "ethnic/gender/
     minority balancing."  They are not opposed to the introduction of
     various ethnic/gender/minority studies courses.  They merely want
     traditional standards of scholarly content to be applied to the
     introduction of those courses (just as with any other courses).  They
     do not believe that ethnic/gender/minority studies courses should be
     adopted solely for the sake of having such courses.

  2) They are opposed to "sensitivity" type courses that are intended to
     indoctrinate students as to acceptable values/beliefs, as opposed to
     having a conventional academic premise.

  3) They want all issues to be open for legitimate debate.  As such, they
     hope to work against attempts to make certain topics "taboo" where
     any attempt to question the conventional wisdom is met by immediate
     charges of racism/misogyny/etc., rather than by any actual discussion.
     (Obviously, this is a much less formal thing, as this has nothing to
     do with any formal school policies.)


From what I could tell, their academic standards were not designed to, in
practice, eliminate all non-traditional courses.  As such, I'm very
skeptical about claims that the NAS is opposing ideas that they find
"politically objectionable."

The only idea that they find "politically objectionable" is the idea that
academic institutions should be used, willy-nilly, to achieve any political
goals that one may have.  What they find academically objectionable is the
idea that "I have a PhD--therefore, my as-yet unfounded beliefs, alone,
form the basis of an academic discipline."


Disclaimer: I am *not* a member of NAS, and as such, I do not represent
            them.  The above is only *my* understanding of their positions.



