Campus Report articles from the archives of Accuracy in Academia (AIA).
Sent independently of AIA by a loyal supporter of the organizations goals and 
purpose.
------------------------------------------------------
 A LAYMAN'S GUIDE TO "POLITICALLY CORRECT" GRAMMAR

 taken from Campus Report, Accuracy In Academia, June 1991

  You can_t play the game if you don_t know the rules.  How can one
 expect to be _politically correct_ if he isn_t up on the P.C.
 terminology?  At his press conference introducing the Collegiate
 Speech Protection Act of 1991, Representative Henry Hyde (R-IL)
 joked with a reporter that if the reporter were any shorter he
 would be considered _vertically-challenged._  His comment was
 followed up by Nadine Strossen, then President-Elect of the
 American Civil Liberties Union, who informing Hyde that his
 observation was a form of _lookism._
  Now, that_s a funny anecdote that not everyone can understand.
 Many loyal Campus Report readers have contacted us wanting to be
 let in on the secret of PC lingo.  Therefore, we are providing
 this partial listing of the new words that are coming into use on
 the college campus and getting a foothold in mainstream
 conversation.
  The usage of _politically correct_ language can be difficult.
 Sometimes, it is grammatically incorrect to add an "anti" to an
 "ism."  Such was the case at the National Lesbian Conference held
 in Atlanta last April (the NLC made national news when organizers
 refused to let a gay journalist cover the event because he was
 male).  The following grammar lesson learned from the NLC came
 from Colleen Marzec_s column, _How to Sound Like a Lesbian,_ that
 appeared in the Washington Blade: _The most frequently used
 suffixes and prefixes in conference literature were 'anti' and
 'ism.'  The prefix 'anti' usually indicates being 'opposed to.'
 The suffix 'ism' has many uses.  In racism and sexism, 'ism'
 indicates a 'system of discrimination' based on race or sex.
 Conference lesbians apparently were 'opposed to' many different
 'systems of discrimination,' such as those based on physical
 abilities, age, sexual orientation, or race.
  _Here are the rules: To create a workshop title [for the
 conference], attach an 'anti' to an 'ism' (Example: anti-albeism,
 anti-ageism, anti-heterosexism, anti-imperialism).  There are
 exceptions.  Anti-violence and anti-oppression don_t quite require
 and 'ism.'  Likewise, youthism and adultism don_t require an
 'anti.'  And some anti-isms, namely the 'oppressions,' don_t
 require a prefix or a suffix, like fat oppression and religious
 oppression._
  The following list of _politically correct_ terminology comes
 from a compilation of terms from the Office of Student Affairs at
 Smith College, Time magazine, and John Leo_s column _The Political
 Taboos of the _90s_ that appeared in the March 4, 1991 U.S. News
 and World Report:
   * Ableism: oppression of the differently-abled by the
 temporarily-able.
   * Adultism: prejudice against juniors; the oppression of
 imposing standards and values derived from the adult world upon
 the culture of subadults.  _Adultism is where adults tell you what
 to do whether its right or wrong,_ according to Ramon Santiago,
 age 20.
   * Ageism: oppression of the young and the old by young adults
 and the middle-aged, in the belief that others are unable to take
 care of themselves.
   * Classism: oppression of the working and non-propertied classes
 by the upper- and middle-classes.
   * Colonialism: oppression of people of Third World heritage;
 including oppression of native peoples or immigrants from the
 Third World by people of European heritage.
   * Differently-Abled: handicapped.  Some people like to refer to
 people without handicaps as _the temporarily-able._
   * Ethnocentrism: oppression of cultures other than the dominant
 one in the belief that the dominant way of doing things is the
 superior way.
   * Eurocentrism: failure to see all the world_s culture as equal
 in the development of freedom, democracy, science, literature,
 technology, medicine, and the creation of wealth.
   * Heterosexism: oppression of those of a sexual orientation
 other than heterosexual.
   * Laughism: _inappropriately-directed laughter,_ which has been
 banned at the University of Connecticut.
   * Lookism: the belief that appearance is an indicator of a
 person_s value; the construction of a standard for
 beauty/attractiveness; and oppression through stereotypes and
 generalizations of both those who do not fit the standard and
 those who do.
   * Militarism: the love of war.
   * Persons of Color: blacks, Hispanics, Asians, and so on.
   * Racism: the belief that one group of people are superior to
 another and therefore have the right to dominate.
   * Religious discrimination: oppression of religions other than
 the dominant one.
   * Sexism: stereotyping of males and female on the basis of their
 gender; the oppression of women by society in the belief that
 gender is an indication of ability.
   * Specieism: the doctrine or feeling that humans are somehow
 more valuable than mice or insects.  Like sexists, _speciesists
 allow the interests of their own species to override the greater
 interests of members of other species,_ writes Peter Singer,
 philosopher of animal rights.
   * Vertically-Challenged: too short or too tall.
-----------------------------------------------------

                    WHAT IS AIA?
     Accuracy in Academia, a nonprofit public service organization based in 
Washington, D.C., seeks the reassertion of traditional academic ethics in our 
universities. We contend that academic freedom is threatened by a progressive 
ideological orthodoxy, pervasive in the intellectual community, which degrades 
professional standards in teaching and scholarship, and inhibits speech and 
research which contradict orthodox views.
     AIA wishes to see the academy return to its proper mission: the quest for 
truth. To promote our goal, we document and publicize political bias within the
academy in our monthly newspaper, "Campus Report". Our articles focus on three 
issues: the exploitation of the classroom or university resources to 
indoctrinate students; discrimination against students, faculty or 
administrators based on political or academic beliefs; and campus violations of
free speech.
